## C. Companion richness models
Poisson rejected (Pearson dispersion 2.13, Cameron–Johnson DJ z = 19.7, p < 1e-85); NB2 used
(alpha ≈ 1.28). Hurdle split: the effect is entirely in the **presence** part (beta 5.13, p=0.0003),
count part n.s. (p=0.43).

| response | beta(delta_urban_1993_2009.500m) | p | IRR per +10pp | 95% CI |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| n.total | +2.078 | 0.0083 | 1.231 | 1.055–1.436 |
| **n.unint** | **+3.127** | **0.0003** | **1.367** | 1.154–1.620 |
| n.gard | -0.047 | 0.957 | 0.995 | 0.838–1.181 |
| n.agfo (descriptive only) | +1.037 | 0.653 | 1.109 | 0.706–1.743 |
| n.unint, excl. Urban habitat | +3.577 | 0.0045 | 1.430 | 1.117–1.831 |
| n.total, excl. Urban habitat | +2.308 | 0.0325 | 1.260 | 1.019–1.557 |

Zero-trial handling: 442/632 plots have n.gard+n.unint = 0. A binomial term with 0 trials
contributes exactly 0 to the log-likelihood — verified numerically
(logLik all 632 = logLik 190 = -294.9387837038, difference 0.00e+00), so the compositional
model is **not** distorted by the zero plots; it is also not a hurdle question.

